{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Remember the Sabbath day, to sanctify it.</b> After He commanded that we believe in HaShem, the unified One, blessed be He, that He is the Existent, that He is the Creator, that He is the One who understands, and that He is the One who is able, and that we unify the faith in all of these and that the honor is His alone, and He commanded that we honor the remembrance of His name, He commanded that we make in this a sign and a perpetual remembrance to make known that He created everything — and this is the commandment of the Sabbath, for it is a remembrance of the work of Creation.<br>And He said, <b>Remember the Sabbath day, to sanctify it,</b> and in the repetition of the Torah (Deuteronomy 5:12) it is written: \"Observe the Sabbath day, to sanctify it.\" And our Sages said of this (Rosh HaShanah 27a): \"Remember\" and \"Observe\" were spoken in a single utterance, and they did not insist on the other expressions that were changed for them. And the intention of the Sages, may their memory be blessed, is that \"Remember\" is a positive commandment — He commanded that we remember the Sabbath day to sanctify it and not forget it — while \"Observe\" in their usage is a negative commandment, for everywhere that השמר (take heed), פן (lest), or אל (do not) is stated, it is nothing but a negative commandment (Eruvin 96a): He warns that we observe it to sanctify it, that we not profane it. And it would not be fitting for Moses to exchange the words of HaShem from a positive commandment to a negative commandment. But if he exchanged in the second version, \"all manner\" (כל תמונה) and said \"all manner\" (כל תמונה) — missing the vav — and added it in \"and on the third day\" (וביום השלישי), and so everything of this sort in the other Decalogue statements, there is nothing in it, for it is all one. And this reasoning would be tolerated only by one who is not accustomed to the Talmud. For it is explicitly stated (Berakhot 20b): Women are obligated in the sanctification of the day by Torah law, as it is said: \"Remember\" and \"Observe\" — everyone who is included in \"observance\" is included in \"remembrance.\" And these women, since they are included in \"observance,\" are included in \"remembrance\" — for women are obligated in \"observance,\" since they are obligated in every negative commandment, and they would not have been obligated in \"remembrance,\" which is a positive commandment that is time-bound and from which women are exempt, except that this pairing obligates them. And I marvel: if \"Remember\" and \"Observe\" were spoken by the Almighty, why were they not written on the first tablets? And it is possible that on the first tablets and on the second, \"Remember\" was written, and Moses explained to Israel that \"Observe\" was spoken with it. And this is truly their intention.<br>And in the midrash of Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah (Sefer HaBahir, siman 182) they further alluded to a great mystery in \"Remember\" and \"Observe,\" and in general: the remembrance shall be by day and the observance by night. And this is the statement of the Sages (Bava Kamma 32a) who say on Sabbath eve: \"Come, O bride; come, O bride\" — \"Come let us go to meet the Sabbath, the queen, the bride\" — and they call the blessing of the day Qiddusha Rabba (Pesachim 106a), which is the great sanctification, and you will understand this. And it is also true that the attribute of \"Remember\" they alluded to in a positive commandment, and it proceeds from the attribute of Love, and it belongs to the attribute of Mercy, for one who fulfills the commandment of his Master is beloved by Him and his Master has mercy upon him. And the attribute of \"Observe\" is in a negative commandment, and it belongs to the attribute of Judgment and proceeds from the attribute of Fear, for one who guards himself from doing what is evil in the eyes of his Master fears Him. And therefore a positive commandment is greater than a negative commandment, just as love is greater than fear, for one who fulfills and does with his body and his property the will of his Master is greater than one who merely guards himself from what is evil in His eyes. And therefore they said that a positive commandment comes and overrides a negative commandment, and because of this the punishment for a negative commandment is greater, and judgment is applied to it, such as lashes and death, whereas no judgment at all is applied to a positive commandment except in the case of the rebellious one — for example, one who says, \"I will not take the lulav,\" \"I will not wear tzitzit,\" \"I will not dwell in a sukkah\" — whom the Sanhedrin would beat until he undertakes to do it, or until his soul departs.<br>And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Exodus 20:8) in explanation of \"Remember\": \"Give heed to remember the Sabbath day always, so that if you chance upon a fine portion, set it aside for the Sabbath.\" And this is a baraita that is taught in the Mekhilta (ad loc.) thus: Rabbi Elazar ben Chananya ben Chizkiyah ben Guron says: \"Remember the Sabbath day, to sanctify it\" — you should remember it from one day before the Sabbath, so that if you chance upon a fine item, you should prepare it for the Sabbath. But it is taught in singular language, and it is not the halakhah, for in the Gemara (Beitzah 16a) they said: It was taught, they said of Shammai the Elder: all his days he would eat in honor of the Sabbath. How? If he found a fine beast, he would say: \"This is for the honor of the Sabbath.\" The next day he found another, finer one; he would set aside the second and eat the first. But Hillel the Elder had a different trait: all his deeds were for the sake of Heaven, as it is said (Psalms 68:20): \"Blessed be YHWH, day by day.\" It was likewise taught: Beit Shammai say: \"Assign for your Sabbath from the first day of the week,\" and Beit Hillel say: \"Blessed be YHWH, day by day.\"<br>And in another Mekhilta (Mekhilta de-Rashbi) — Shammai the Elder says: remembrance before it arrives, observance once it has arrived. And there was a deed of Shammai the Elder: the remembrance of the Sabbath never departed from his mouth. He would pick up a fine item and say: \"This is for the Sabbath.\" A new vessel — he would say: \"This is for the Sabbath.\" But Hillel the Elder had a different trait, for he would say: \"Let all your deeds be for the sake of Heaven.\" And the halakhah follows Beit Hillel. And according to the plain sense, they said (in the Mekhilta, ad loc.) that it is a commandment to remember the Sabbath constantly, every day, that we not forget it and that it not be confused for us among the other days, for by remembering it always, we will remember the work of Creation at all times, and we will acknowledge at all times that the world has a Creator, and He commanded us in this sign, as He said (Exodus 31:13): \"for it is a sign between Me and you,\" and this is a great principle in the faith in El.<br>And the meaning of <b>to sanctify it</b> is that our remembrance of it should be such that it is holy in our eyes, as it is said: \"And you shall call the Sabbath a delight, the holy day of YHWH, honored\" (Isaiah 58:13). And the meaning is: the cessation of labor should be in our eyes on account of its being a holy day, to turn aside in it from the preoccupations of thought and the vanities of the times, and to take in it delight for our souls in the ways of HaShem, and to go to the sages and to the prophets to hear the words of HaShem, as it is said (II Kings 4:23): \"Why do you go to him today? It is neither new moon nor Sabbath\" — for such was their practice, and thus our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Rosh HaShanah 16a): by inference, on new moon and Sabbath one must go. And this is the reason for the rest of the beast — that there should be no thought about it in our heart. And therefore our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Chulin 5a) that the Sabbath is equivalent to all the commandments in the Torah, as they said in Avodah Zarah, because through it we testify to all the principles of faith — in Creation ex nihilo, in Providence, and in Prophecy. And in the Mekhilta (ad loc.) Rabbi Yitzchak says: You should not count as the others count, but you should count for the sake of the Sabbath. And its explanation is: the nations count the days of the week for the sake of the days themselves, calling each day by its own name, or after the names of the celestial attendants, as the Christians do, or other names they may call them; and Israel counts all the days for the sake of the Sabbath — \"one day toward the Sabbath,\" \"second day toward the Sabbath\" — for this is part of the commandment to remember it constantly every day. And this is the plain sense of the verse, and so Rabbi Elazar explained it. And I say that this is the midrash of Shammai the Elder (in Mekhilta de-Rashbi), who explained the commandment of \"Remember\" as \"before it arrives\" — that is, that we not forget it under any circumstances — but in the baraita (Beitzah 16a) they further mentioned his trait of piety, that he himself would also remember it in his foods and eat in honor of the Sabbath all the days of his life. And Hillel himself concedes in the midrash of Shammai, but he had a different trait regarding foods, because all his deeds were for the sake of Heaven, and he trusted in HaShem that He would provide him for the Sabbath a finer portion than all the other days.<br>But our Sages further derive from the word \"to sanctify it\" — that we should sanctify it with a recitation, in the manner of \"And you shall sanctify the fiftieth year\" (Leviticus 25:10), which requires sanctification by the court, who say regarding the Jubilee: \"It is sanctified, it is sanctified.\" So too here, He commanded that we remember the Sabbath day by sanctifying it. And thus they said in the Mekhilta (ad loc.): \"To sanctify it\" — sanctify it with a blessing. From here they said: one recites the sanctification over wine at its inception. I have only the day; from where do I derive the night? Scripture says: \"And you shall observe the Sabbath\" (Exodus 31:14). And this is the sanctification of the day, and it is from the Torah — it is not an asmakhta. And thus they said (Berakhot 20b): Women are obligated in the sanctification of the day by Torah law — and this refers to the sanctification of the night, because all things that require sanctification are sanctified at their inception once, such as the sanctification of the new month and the sanctification of the Jubilee. But for the day it is an asmakhta, and one does not say \"it is sanctified\" at all, for it suffices us with once, at its inception. And likewise the requirement of wine is an asmakhta and is not obligatory at all. And in the Gemara, Pesachim (106a), they said: <b>Remember the Sabbath day, to sanctify it</b> — remember it over wine at its inception. I have only the day; from where the night? Scripture says: <b>the Sabbath day.</b> That tanna turns to the night and derives for it a verse from the day — and moreover, the principal sanctification is at night. Rather, this is what he says: <b>Remember the Sabbath day, to sanctify it</b> — remember it over wine at its inception. I have only the night; from where the day? Scripture says: <b>the Sabbath day.</b> And likewise the baraita in the Mekhilta resolves it as: I have only the night, which is the principal sanctification; from where the day? etc. — and it is merely an asmakhta. And from there you learn that this commandment is derived from the word \"to sanctify it,\" but <b>Remember the Sabbath day</b> is a commandment to remember it constantly every day, as we have explained. Only, all the commandments of remembrance are counted as a single one in the reckoning of 248 commandments that we were commanded. And know this."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :register {:flags {0 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {0 "The word תמונה appears in both versions of the Decalogue (Exodus 20:10 and Deuteronomy 5:14); Ramban's point about the missing vav refers to a known variant in the spelling of כל תמונה between the two versions. The reading here is hyper-literal and the argument turns on orthographic minutiae that are not fully spelled out."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:18:09.445572327Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}